//? Title: Hopfield memory: actual asynchronous recall and its limits
//? Subtitle: Hebbian storage, recorded local fields, energy plateaus and exact versus bitwise recovery
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Store bipolar patterns by normalized Hebbian outer products, then update one
neuron at a time from its current local field. A Hopfield memory can recover a
corrupted pattern, but need not return the nearest supplied pattern or even keep
every stored pattern fixed. We start with three neurons, retain the original
hundred-neuron experiment and all 330 noise trials, and inspect producing sweep
orders, sign decisions and energy observations. Ten sampled corruption toggles
are distinguished from actual Hamming distance; mean bit agreement is
distinguished from exact whole-pattern recovery. Zero-field plateaus, a
correlated-memory failure and an unstored reversed attractor define the limits
of the energy argument.
)

\toc

> Three neurons: one wrong sign and one actual flip

Store the bipolar memory (1,-1,1). With N=3, its off-diagonal weights are
-1/3,+1/3,-1/3 for pairs 01,02,12; the symmetric entries agree and the diagonal
is zero. Present probe (1,1,1). Neuron 1 has local field -2/3 and flips to -1,
while the other neurons retain +1. Energy changes from 1/3 to -1. The next
complete sweep changes nothing and certifies a fixed point of the computed
update rule.

The table records every actual decision in the first producing sweep. The
diagram's field and state labels come from that same execution. The shuffled
order is an implementation result, not a prescribed left-to-right drawing order.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hopfield_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const auto net = dm::algorithms::hopfield_train_checked({{1, -1, 1}});
    const auto t =
        dm::algorithms::hopfield_recall_trace(net, {1, 1, 1}, 100, 0);
    std::print("| actual first-sweep neuron | local field | sign before | sign after |\n| ---: | ---: | " "---: | ---: |\n");
    for (const auto &u : t.sweeps.front().updates)
        std::print("| {} | {:.9f} | {} | {} |\n", u.neuron, u.field, u.before,
                   u.after);
    const auto changed =
        std::find_if(t.sweeps.front().updates.begin(), t.sweeps.front().updates.end(), [](const auto &u) {
        return u.before != u.after;
    });
    if (changed == t.sweeps.front().updates.end())
        throw std::runtime_error("missing producing flip");
    std::print("\nActual order {}; initial energy {:.9f}; final energy {:.9f}; actual sweeps {}; no-change " "stop {}; capture units {}.\n",
               t.sweeps.front().order, t.initial_energy,
               t.sweeps.back().energy_after, t.run.sweeps,
               t.run.stop == dm::algorithms::HopfieldStop::NoChange,
               t.captured_entries);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d(65, 1100);
    dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;
    style.width = 4.7;
    style.height = 2.8;
    style.font_size = 21;
    d.node("input", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual probe\n{}\nE = {:.3f}", t.initial_state,
                       t.initial_energy), style).node("field", {7, 0},
                                                      std::format(
                                                      "Neuron {} field\n{:.3f}\n{} becomes {}",
                                                      changed->neuron,
                                                      changed->field,
                                                      changed->before,
                                                      changed->after), style).node("out", {14, 0}, std::format("Actual recalled state\n{}\nE = {:.3f}", t.run.state, t.sweeps.back().energy_after), style).edge("input", "field", "current state").edge("field", "out", "asynchronous flip").text({7, 3}, "One producing sign change; then an unchanged sweep", 24).save_svg("build/hopfield_tiny.svg");
    if (t.run.state != std::vector<int>{1, -1, 1} || t.run.work.flips != 1)
        throw std::runtime_error("tiny recall");
}
